
NOTES 03/19:

@Component(...): An instance of class (a spring bean) will be managed by Spring framework
@Dependecy: GameRunner needs GamingConsole implementation
@ComponentScan: Specify package names to scan it and it's sub packages for components
DependencyInjection: Identify beans, their dependencies, and wire them together (provides IOC - Inversion of Control)
	Spring Beans: An object managed by Spring Framework
 	IoC Container: Manages the life cycle of beans and dependencies
 		Types: ApplicationContext (complex), BeanFactory (simpler features - rarely used)
 	Autowiring: Process of wiring in dependencies for a Spring Bean
 	
 	
@Component vs @Bean

@Component
	- Can be used on any Java class
	- Very easy, just need to add annotation
	- Autowiring very easy, just use field, setter, or constructor injection
	- Spring Framework creates the beans
	- Better for instantiating Beans for Application code @Component
	- 
	
@Bean
	- Typically used on methods in Spring Configuration classes
	- You need to write all the code to create the bean and auto wire dependencies
	- Autowiring easy, use a method call or method parameters
	- Developer creates the bean creation code
	- Better for Custom Business Logic and instantiating beans for 3rd party libraries @Bean
	